{
  "name": "What to Do When Your Under-Sink Clearance Isn't Enough",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "It's frustrating to realize that your newly installed sink doesn't provide the necessary knee and toe clearance, especially for wheelchair users. This can often happen if measurements aren't precise or if plumbing gets in the way. First, assess where the obstruction is coming from. Is it the pipes themselves, or perhaps a support structure? You need a minimum of 30 inches wide and 17 to 25 inches deep for knee and toe space, with at least 27 inches of knee height. If pipes are the issue, sometimes a skilled plumber can reroute them, but it’s not always possible without significant work. If the sink's apron or depth is the problem, you might need a different sink altogether. Don't assume a product spec guarantees compliance; it's all about the installed condition. If you're dealing with accessibility requirements, especially in a remodel or commercial setting, getting professional input from someone like HH Remodeling LLC early on can save you a lot of headaches and rework later.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    }
  ],
